.TH SLZ 1 "November 2026" "libslz 1.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
slz, unslz, slzcat \- fast gzip, zlib and deflate compression and decompression
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B slz
.RI [ option ]...
.RI [ file ]...
.br
.B slz \-d
.RI [ option ]...
.RI [ file ]...
.br
.B slz \-dc
.RI [ option ]...
.RI [ file ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B slz
is the command-line utility of libslz, a library which compresses streams with
very little memory and CPU usage, mainly used by servers to compress their
responses on the fly. It uses the library's compressor and decompressor, and
the project page and sources given below are those of the library.
.PP
.B slz
compresses data into the gzip format (RFC1952), the zlib format (RFC1950) or
raw deflate (RFC1951), the formats that web browsers and gzip-compatible tools
understand. It focuses on speed: even at its best level it is several times
faster than
.BR gzip (1),
for a somewhat larger output. It is well suited to compressing large amounts
of data on the fly, such as backups sent over a network, logs, or archives,
when the time spent compressing matters more than the last percent of
compression ratio.
.PP
It also decompresses any gzip, zlib or raw deflate stream, not only those it
produced, and is usually faster than
.BR zcat (1)
at it.
.PP
It is used like
.BR gzip (1).
Given file names, each
.I file
is compressed in place: the output is written to the same name followed by
the extension of the format
.RB ( .gz
for gzip, the default,
.B .zz
for zlib and
.B .dfl
for raw deflate), it receives the mode, owner and times of
.IR file ,
and
.I file
is then removed. The name of the file is not stored in the output.
.PP
With
.BR \-d ,
each
.I file
is decompressed in place the same way, to its name without its extension,
whichever it is. The gzip and zlib formats are recognized from the data
themselves. Raw deflate cannot be recognized, so it is selected by the
.B .dfl
extension, or by
.BR \-D .
A file without an extension can only be decompressed to the standard output
.RB ( \-c ),
since there would be no name for the output.
.PP
Without any file name, or when the name is
.BR \- ,
the standard input is processed and the result is written to the standard
output.
.PP
An output file is only created once its input could be opened, and an
existing output is only replaced with
.BR \-f .
On error or interruption, the partial output is removed and the input is
kept.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-1 " ... " \-9
Compression level.
.B \-1
is the fastest,
.B \-2
is the default, and
.B \-4
compresses the most; levels above 4 are the same as
.BR \-4 .
.B \-0
does not compress at all and only wraps the data into the selected format.
.TP
.B \-c
Write the result to the standard output and keep the input files. This is the
default when no file name is given. When several files are given, their
compressed streams are concatenated. In gzip format, gzip tools, including
.BR "slz \-d" ,
decompress such a concatenation as a whole.
.TP
.B \-d
Decompress instead of compressing.
.TP
.B \-f
Force: replace existing output files, compress symbolic links and files which
already have the extension, and accept to write compressed data to a terminal
or to read it from one.
.TP
.B \-k
Keep the input files instead of removing them.
.TP
.B \-t
Test: process the inputs without writing anything. With
.BR \-d ,
this checks the integrity of compressed files: the exit status tells whether
they could all be decompressed.
.TP
.B \-v
Report the sizes, the compression ratio and the speed for each input.
.TP
.B \-h
Display a short help and exit.
.TP
.B \-n
Does nothing, accepted for compatibility with
.BR gzip (1).
.TP
.B \-G
Use the gzip format, with the
.B .gz
extension. This is the default when compressing.
.TP
.B \-Z
Use the zlib format, with the
.B .zz
extension.
.TP
.B \-D
Use the raw deflate format, with the
.B .dfl
extension.
.PP
With
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-G ,
.B \-Z
and
.B \-D
force the format of the inputs instead of recognizing it, whatever their
extension.
.SS Advanced options
.TP
.BI \-K " size"
Block size used when compressing, 4 MB by default. When decompressing, size of
the output buffer, 32 kB by default, which is also the minimum. The size is in
kilobytes, or followed by a
.BR b ,
.BR k ,
.B m
or
.B g
suffix.
.TP
.B \-m
Read the input into a buffer of the block size instead of mapping it into
memory. This is done automatically for pipes and other inputs which cannot be
mapped.
.TP
.BI \-b " size"
Only use the first
.I size
bytes of each input, with
.B \-c
or
.B \-t
only.
.TP
.BI \-l " loops"
With
.BR \-t ,
process each input
.I loops
times, to measure the speed with
.BR \-v .
.TP
.B \-p
With
.BR \-t ,
load the input and the buffers into memory before starting, so that the
measurement excludes it.
.SH OTHER NAMES
When invoked under the name
.B unslz
or
.BR gunzip ,
.B slz
behaves as
.BR "slz \-d" ,
and when invoked as
.BR slzcat ,
.BR zcat ,
.B gzcat
or
.BR zdec ,
it behaves as
.BR "slz \-dc" .
The name
.B zenc
is its former name and compresses as usual. These names only need a symbolic
link to
.BR slz .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when all inputs were processed successfully, 1 when at least one of them
could not be, or on a usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Compress a file to
.IR file.gz ,
removing
.IR file :
.PP
.RS
slz file
.RE
.PP
Decompress it back, removing
.IR file.gz :
.PP
.RS
slz \-d file.gz
.RE
.PP
Compress a directory with
.BR tar (1)
at the fastest level, and send it over the network:
.PP
.RS
tar cf \- dir | slz \-1 | ssh host 'cat > dir.tar.gz'
.RE
.PP
Read a compressed log without decompressing it to disk:
.PP
.RS
slz \-dc access.log.gz | less
.RE
.PP
Check the integrity of all the compressed files of a directory:
.PP
.RS
slz \-dt *.gz && echo OK
.RE
.PP
Compress a file in zlib format, keeping the original:
.PP
.RS
slz \-Z \-k data.json
.RE
.PP
Measure the compression speed and ratio at each level, on a large file:
.PP
.RS
for l in 1 2 3 4; do slz \-t \-v \-$l file.tar; done
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gzip (1),
.BR zcat (1),
.BR tar (1)
.PP
